The Battle of Buxar, fought on 22 October 1764 near the town of Buxar in present-day Bihar, was one of the most decisive battles in Indian history and a major turning point in the establishment of British rule in India. It was fought between the army of the British East India Company, commanded by Hector Munro, and a powerful alliance of three Indian rulers: Mir Qasim, the Nawab of Bengal; Shuja-ud-Daula, the Nawab of Awadh; and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II. The roots of the conflict lay in the growing tensions between the Company and Mir Qasim, who had initially been installed as Nawab by the British after the Battle of Plassey. However, unlike his predecessor Mir Jafar, Mir Qasim proved to be an ambitious and capable ruler who sought to free Bengal from British interference. He attempted to reform the administration, modernize his army, and most importantly abolish internal trade duties in order to create equality between Indian merchants and Company officials. The British, who were misusing their duty-free trade privileges and causing heavy revenue losses to the Bengal treasury, strongly opposed these reforms. As relations deteriorated, open conflict erupted, forcing Mir Qasim to flee and form an alliance with Shuja-ud-Daula and Shah Alam II to challenge British authority.

Despite the allied forces being numerically larger, the Company’s army was better trained, more disciplined, and strategically organized. The battle lasted only a few hours but ended in a decisive victory for the British. The defeat of the combined Indian forces not only crushed organized resistance in eastern India but also demonstrated the military superiority of the Company. The consequences of the battle were far-reaching: in 1765, the Treaty of Allahabad was signed, by which Shah Alam II granted the Company the Diwani rights, or the authority to collect revenue, in Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa. This grant provided the Company with enormous financial resources and marked the beginning of dual administration in Bengal. Unlike Plassey, which had merely established British political influence, the Battle of Buxar firmly consolidated British power and transformed the East India Company from a commercial trading body into a territorial and political power. It laid the economic and administrative foundation for the expansion of British rule across India and significantly weakened the Mughal Empire, accelerating the shift of power from Indian rulers to colonial authority.
